.TH CLRUCACHE 7 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
clrucache \- overview of the thread-safe LRU cache
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A clru_cache holds up to a fixed number of key-value entries and
evicts the least recently used entry to make room for a new one. A
hash map gives it O(1) lookup and a linked list O(1) eviction. A cache
can sit in front of a remote store: a remote getter fills a miss, and
a remote setter writes each set through to the store before the cache
changes.

The API has two layers:
  - The type-inferred macros clru_construct(3), clru_init(3),
    clru_get(3), clru_set(3) and clru_destroy(3) keep the key type and
    the value type with the handle and convert each argument to them.
  - The raw functions clrucache_create_full(3), clrucache_get_full(3)
    and clrucache_set_full(3) take each key and each value as a
    cmap_pair and return a ccol_retval_t.
.fi
.SS "Handles"
.nf
clru_cache is an opaque value handle, not a pointer, and
CLRU_CACHE_INVALID (0) means no cache. Every handle is checked before
the cache is touched, so a get or a set through a handle that is
invalid or already destroyed reports ccol_invalid_args, and a destroy
of a stale handle stops the process. clru_destroy(3) evicts every
entry, calling the eviction callback for each one, and sets the handle
to CLRU_CACHE_INVALID.
.fi
.SS "Segments and eviction"
.nf
Below a capacity of 128 a cache is one segment with one exact
least-recently-used order. From 128 entries up the cache is divided
into segments, each with its own lock. A hash of the key picks the
segment, and each segment evicts its own least recently used entry
when its share of the capacity is full, so a full segmented cache
holds a few percent less than its capacity.
clrucache_create_full(3) has the details.
.fi
.SS "Ownership"
.nf
The cache keeps its own copy of every key and value. A get never hands
out a pointer into the cache: clru_get(3) copies the value into the
caller's variable, and clrucache_get_full(3) gives a heap copy that
the caller frees with the cache's allocator (free(3) when the cache
has none). A char * value that clru_get(3) delivers is such a heap
copy too.

A remote getter allocates the value it fetches with the cache's
allocator, and the cache takes ownership of it. The cache keeps its
own copy of the ccol_memmgmt_procs_t struct, so the caller can release
the struct once the create returns, but the functions it names must
stay callable until the cache is destroyed.

A key of a type that the cache doesn't know, such as a struct, is the
same key as another one only when every byte of the two is equal,
padding included. With the raw functions, zero such a key with
memset(3) before you set its members. With GCC 11 or later the macros
clear the padding of their own copy of the key.
.fi
.SS "Concurrent operations on one key"
.nf
Getters that miss on the same key share one remote fetch: one getter
runs the remote getter, and the others wait and get its result.
Setters of the same key run one at a time, and a getter of a key waits
while a set of that key is in progress.

A set takes effect when its remote setter returns. Every step that can
fail for lack of memory runs before the remote setter, and nothing
after a remote setter that succeeds can fail, so the cache never
serves a value older than the one the remote store holds. A set whose
remote setter fails returns ccol_unexpected_failure, and the key keeps
its old value and its place in the eviction order. While the remote
setter runs the key stays an ordinary entry, so an insert of another
key can evict it.
.fi
.SS "Callbacks"
.nf
The remote getter and the remote setter run with no lock of the cache
held, so they can use the cache for any other key. They must not get
or set the key they serve, and they must not destroy the cache,
because each of those calls waits for the operation that runs the
callback.

The eviction callback runs while the lock of the evicting segment is
held, so it must not call into the cache at all. Two segments can
evict at the same time on two threads, so a callback that keeps state
of its own must protect that state.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Every function and macro of clrucache can be called from many threads
at the same time on one cache. Operations on keys in different
segments don't wait for each other. A destroy waits until every call
running on the cache has returned before it frees anything, and a get
or a set that starts after the destroy reports ccol_invalid_args. Two
destroys of one handle are a programming error.
.fi
